.TH "UBERTOOTH\-SPECAN" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-specan\fR \- a spectrum analyzer for Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-specan [\-l <freq>] [\-u <freq>] [\-g|G] [\-d <filename>|\-]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ubertooth\-specan(1) is a tool for using Ubertooth as a spectrum analyzer in the 2\.4GHz band\. It is often used as a helper for GUI tools, such as ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) or Spectools\. It can also produce output suitable for user with the feedgnuplot tool\.
.P
Other options allow upper and lower bounds to be set on the frequencies monitored\. Refer to the \fIOPTIONS\fR section for full details\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
Options:
.TP
\fB\-l <2402\-2480>\fR
lower frequency in MHz (default 2402); the value is required when \fB\-l\fR is used
.TP
\fB\-u <2402\-2480>\fR
upper frequency in MHz (default 2480); the value is required when \fB\-u\fR is used and must not be lower than the selected lower frequency
.TP
\fB\-g\fR
write the samples as text for feedgnuplot
.TP
\fB\-G\fR
write them as text for 3D feedgnuplot
.TP
\fB\-d <filename>\fR
write them to \fB<filename>\fR instead of the screen; \fB\-d \-\fR writes them to standard output\. Without \fB\-g\fR or \fB\-G\fR they are the raw 3\-byte records, so standard output must not be a terminal\. See ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
report the sweep on standard error; see VERBOSE OUTPUT\. Give it at most once: a second \fB\-v\fR is refused with \fBerror: \-v may be given only once\fR
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
To monitor the 2\.4GHz band and produce a file suitable for feedgnuplot:
.P
ubertooth\-specan \-g \-d output\.dat
.P
To monitor the upper half of the 2\.4GHz band, use:
.P
ubertooth\-specan \-l 2440
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-specan\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: lower frequency must be 2402\-2480 MHz (got bad)\fR\.
.P
An option the tool does not know, and an option whose value is missing, are refused the same way, with the usage text after the \fBerror:\fR line on standard error: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR and \fBerror: option '\-d' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-\fR means standard output only as the whole value of \fB\-d\fR\. Any other value is a file name, including one that starts with \fB\-\fR: \fB\-d \-x\fR writes the file \fB\-x\fR, and a forgotten file name such as \fB\-d \-v\fR writes a file called \fB\-v\fR rather than streaming samples to the terminal and dropping the \fB\-v\fR\.
.P
\fB\-d\fR chooses where the samples go and \fB\-g\fR or \fB\-G\fR how they are written, so the two do separate jobs and go together, in either order: \fB\-g \-d out\.dat\fR writes the gnuplot text into \fBout\.dat\fR, and \fB\-d out\.bin\fR on its own writes the raw records\. \fB\-d\fR used to create the file and leave it empty while a later \fB\-g\fR took the samples to the screen instead\.
.P
One sweep is written one way, so \fB\-g\fR and \fB\-G\fR in one command are refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: \-G cannot be combined with \-g; a sweep is written in one format\fR\. The second one used to be the one used\.
.P
\fB\-d \-\fR is for a pipe or a redirect when the samples are the raw records, which fill a terminal with control characters and can leave it in a strange state, so a terminal is refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write binary samples to it\fR, with exit status 1\. ubertooth\-btle(1) refuses its binary capture to a terminal the same way\. With \fB\-g\fR or \fB\-G\fR the samples are text, and a terminal is welcome to them\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-d\fR names its file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\.
.SH "VERBOSE OUTPUT"
\fB\-v\fR reports what the sweep is doing on standard error\. It writes nothing to standard output, so the samples are the same with it and without it, including the raw records of \fB\-d \-\fR\.
.P
Three lines before the sweep starts name the board, the sweep as it will actually run, and where the samples go:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Using Ubertooth 1ff0ff1967102caf8334fa5ac52000f5
Sweeping 2402\-2480 MHz in 1 MHz steps, 79 samples per sweep
Writing raw samples to standard output
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The board's serial number is read from the board, so it is the one that was selected even when \fB\-U\fR named an enumeration index\. A board that does not answer gives \fBUsing an Ubertooth whose serial number could not be read\fR instead, and the sweep goes on\.
.P
While the sweep runs, one summary line a second, and one more when it ends:
.IP "" 4
.nf
After 1\.0 s: 12 sweeps, 948 samples, 0 out of range
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBsweeps\fR counts the passes over the range that completed, \fBsamples\fR the samples inside the range, and \fBout of range\fR the ones dropped because they were left over from an earlier run, still queued on the board\. The line is printed whether samples arrived or not, so a board that has stopped sending shows up within a second as counters that stop moving\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries only the samples \(em whatever \fB\-d\fR has not sent to a file, as text or, with no \fB\-g\fR or \fB\-G\fR, as the raw records \(em and the usage text for \fB\-h\fR\. Errors, warnings and progress, including \fBUbertooth stopped\fR, go to standard error\. The errors this tool prints itself are one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: /no/such/dir/samples\.bin: No such file or directory\fR for a \fB\-d\fR file that cannot be opened\. One kind of line does not follow that yet: libubertooth's own messages, such as \fBcould not open Ubertooth device\fR\.
.P
If the samples cannot be written, \fBubertooth\-specan\fR says so once and exits 1, rather than reporting every failed write and then exiting 0\. A write that fails while the sweep is running ends it there, since a full disk does not come back:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: writing samples failed: wrote 0 of 3 bytes (<reason>)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A write accepted into a buffer can still fail when that buffer is flushed, so the output is flushed and checked after the sweep as well, whether it went to a \fB\-d\fR file, to standard output with \fB\-d \-\fR, or to standard output as text:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: writing samples failed: could not flush the samples (<reason>)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
One case does not reach either line: when \fB\-d \-\fR writes into a pipe whose reader has gone, the process is killed by \fBSIGPIPE\fR before the failed write returns, so the shell reports status 141\.
